{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}

{%- block title %}Layers — unit.gl{%- endblock %}

{%- block header %}
<p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
<h1>Layering System</h1>
<p class="lead">Semantic z-index management for predictable stacking contexts. Replace magic numbers with meaningful
    layer names.</p>
{%- endblock %}

{%- block content %}

<div class="page--layers">

    <!-- Interactive Demo -->
    <section>
        <h2>Interactive Demo</h2>
        <p>Click the buttons to toggle layers and visualize the stacking order. Layers higher in the stack appear in
            front
            of lower layers.</p>

        <div class="layer-controls">
            {% set layers = [
            {"name": "guides", "z": "9998"},
            {"name": "modal", "z": "7000"},
            {"name": "interface", "z": "4000"},
            {"name": "content", "z": "2000"},
            {"name": "canvas", "z": "-1000"}
            ] %}
            {% for layer in layers %}
            <button class="layer-btn layer-btn--active" id="btn-{{ layer.name }}"
                onclick="toggleLayer('{{ layer.name }}')">{{
                layer.name }}</button>
            {% endfor %}
            <button class="layer-btn" onclick="showAll()">Show All</button>
            <button class="layer-btn" onclick="hideAll()">Hide All</button>
        </div>

        <div class="layer-demo">
            {% for layer in layers %}
            <div class="layer-box z-{{ layer.name }}" id="layer-{{ layer.name }}">
                <span class="layer-box__name">{{ layer.name }}</span>
                <span class="layer-box__value">z("{{ layer.name }}")</span>
                <span class="layer-box__zindex">z-index: {{ layer.z }}</span>
            </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Stack Diagram -->
    <section>
        <h2>Stack Order</h2>
        <p>Visual representation of the layer stack. Elements are ordered from bottom (canvas) to top (guides). Higher
            z-index values appear in front.</p>

        <div class="stack-diagram">
            <div class="stack-item stack-item--canvas">
                <span>canvas</span>
                <span class="stack-item__z">z-index: -1000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stack-item stack-item--content">
                <span>content</span>
                <span class="stack-item__z">z-index: 2000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stack-item stack-item--interface">
                <span>interface</span>
                <span class="stack-item__z">z-index: 4000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stack-item stack-item--modal">
                <span>modal</span>
                <span class="stack-item__z">z-index: 7000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stack-item stack-item--guides">
                <span>guides</span>
                <span class="stack-item__z">z-index: 9998</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <section>
        <h2>Overview</h2>

        <p>Managing z-index values across a large project quickly becomes chaotic. The common approach of using
            arbitrary
            numbers like <code>z-index: 9999</code> leads to an arms race where values keep increasing without any logic
            or
            system.</p>

        <p>unit.gl solves this with a <strong>semantic layering system</strong>. Instead of numbers, you use meaningful
            names that describe the <em>purpose</em> of each layer. The <code>z()</code> function maps these names to
            carefully structured z-index values.</p>

        <div class="code-block">@use "unit.gl" as *;

            <span class="comment">// Instead of this...</span>
            .modal { z-index: 9999; }
            .header { z-index: 999; }
            .dropdown { z-index: 100; }

            <span class="comment">// Use semantic names</span>
            .modal { z-index: z("modal"); } <span class="comment">// 7000</span>
            .header { z-index: z("interface"); } <span class="comment">// 4000</span>
            .dropdown { z-index: z("content"); } <span class="comment">// 2000</span>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Layer Stack -->
    <section>
        <h2>The Layer Stack</h2>

        <p>The default layer stack provides five semantic layers with well-spaced z-index values. The large gaps
            (1000–3000
            units) between layers allow room for relative positioning without conflicts.</p>

        <div class="layer-stack">
            <div class="layer-stack__item layer-stack__item--guides">
                <div class="layer-stack__name">guides</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__value">z-index: 9998</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__desc">Development overlays, grid guides, debug tools</div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-stack__item layer-stack__item--modal">
                <div class="layer-stack__name">modal</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__value">z-index: 7000</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__desc">Modal dialogs, lightboxes, fullscreen overlays</div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-stack__item layer-stack__item--interface">
                <div class="layer-stack__name">interface</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__value">z-index: 4000</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__desc">Fixed navigation, sticky headers, toolbars</div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-stack__item layer-stack__item--content">
                <div class="layer-stack__name">content</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__value">z-index: 2000</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__desc">Elevated content, cards, dropdowns, tooltips</div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-stack__item layer-stack__item--canvas">
                <div class="layer-stack__name">canvas</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__value">z-index: −1000</div>
                <div class="layer-stack__desc">Background elements, decorative patterns</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The z() Function -->
    <section>
        <h2>The z() Function</h2>

        <div class="api-signature">z($layer)</div>

        <p>Returns the z-index value for the specified layer name.</p>

        <h3>Parameters</h3>

        <div class="api-param">
            <code>$layer</code>
            <span class="api-param__type">String</span>
            <span class="api-param__desc">The name of the layer (e.g., "modal", "interface", "content")</span>
        </div>

        <h3>Returns</h3>
        <p><code>Number</code> — The z-index value associated with the layer, or <code>null</code> if not found.</p>

        <h3>Example</h3>

        <div class="code-block">@use "unit.gl" as *;

            .modal-overlay {
            position: fixed;
            inset: 0;
            z-index: z("modal"); <span class="comment">// 7000</span>
            }

            .sticky-header {
            position: sticky;
            top: 0;
            z-index: z("interface"); <span class="comment">// 4000</span>
            }

            .card {
            position: relative;
            z-index: z("content"); <span class="comment">// 2000</span>
            }

            .background-pattern {
            position: absolute;
            z-index: z("canvas"); <span class="comment">// -1000</span>
            }</div>
    </section>

    <!-- Layer Reference -->
    <section>
        <h2>Layer Reference</h2>

        <table class="table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Layer</th>
                    <th>z-index</th>
                    <th>Purpose</th>
                    <th>Common Uses</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>guides</code></td>
                    <td>9998</td>
                    <td>Development overlays</td>
                    <td>Grid guides, alignment helpers, debug panels</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>guide--baseline</code></td>
                    <td>9998</td>
                    <td>Baseline grid overlay</td>
                    <td>Typography alignment guides</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>guide--graph</code></td>
                    <td>9998</td>
                    <td>Graph grid overlay</td>
                    <td>Spatial grid visualization</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>modal</code></td>
                    <td>7000</td>
                    <td>Modal dialogs</td>
                    <td>Lightboxes, confirmations, image galleries</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>ribbon</code></td>
                    <td>5000</td>
                    <td>Banners and ribbons</td>
                    <td>Top banners, announcement bars</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>interface</code></td>
                    <td>4000</td>
                    <td>Fixed UI elements</td>
                    <td>Sticky headers, navigation bars, toolbars</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>control</code></td>
                    <td>3000</td>
                    <td>Interactive controls</td>
                    <td>Buttons, sliders, form controls</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>content</code></td>
                    <td>2000</td>
                    <td>Elevated content</td>
                    <td>Cards, dropdowns, tooltips, popovers</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>canvas</code></td>
                    <td>−1000</td>
                    <td>Background layer</td>
                    <td>Page backgrounds, decorative elements</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>max</code></td>
                    <td>9999</td>
                    <td>Maximum layer</td>
                    <td>Use sparingly for critical overlays</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>mid</code></td>
                    <td>0</td>
                    <td>Baseline</td>
                    <td>Default stacking position</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>min</code></td>
                    <td>−9999</td>
                    <td>Minimum layer</td>
                    <td>Force elements behind everything</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>

    <!-- Relative Positioning -->
    <section>
        <h2>Relative Positioning</h2>

        <p>Sometimes you need elements to stack relative to a layer rather than exactly on it. Use simple arithmetic
            with
            the <code>z()</code> function:</p>

        <div class="code-block"><span class="comment">// Dropdown trigger and menu</span>
            .dropdown-trigger {
            z-index: z("content"); <span class="comment">// 2000</span>
            }

            .dropdown-menu {
            z-index: z("content") + 10; <span class="comment">// 2010</span>
            }

            <span class="comment">// Modal with backdrop</span>
            .modal-backdrop {
            z-index: z("modal") - 1; <span class="comment">// 6999</span>
            }

            .modal-dialog {
            z-index: z("modal"); <span class="comment">// 7000</span>
            }

            <span class="comment">// Nested cards</span>
            .card { z-index: z("content"); }
            .card__overlay { z-index: z("content") + 1; }
            .card__actions { z-index: z("content") + 2; }
        </div>

        <div class="alert alert--tip">
            <p class="alert__title">Tip</p>
            <p>Use small offsets (+1, +5, +10) for related elements. The large gaps between layers (1000–3000 units)
                give
                you plenty of room.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Customization -->
    <section>
        <h2>Customization</h2>

        <p>You can extend or override the default layer map to fit your project's needs:</p>

        <div class="code-block"><span class="comment">// Override $layer_map before importing unit.gl</span>
            $layer_map: (
            "canvas": -1000,
            "background": 0,
            "content": 2000,
            "dropdown": 3000, <span class="comment">// Custom layer</span>
            "sticky": 3500, <span class="comment">// Custom layer</span>
            "interface": 4000,
            "overlay": 5000, <span class="comment">// Custom layer</span>
            "modal": 7000,
            "toast": 8000, <span class="comment">// Custom layer</span>
            "tooltip": 8500, <span class="comment">// Custom layer</span>
            "guides": 9998,
            );

            @use "unit.gl" as * with ($layer_map: $layer_map);

            <span class="comment">// Now use your custom layers</span>
            .toast-notification {
            z-index: z("toast"); <span class="comment">// 8000</span>
            }

            .tooltip {
            z-index: z("tooltip"); <span class="comment">// 8500</span>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Best Practices -->
    <section>
        <h2>Best Practices</h2>

        <div class="best-practices">
            <div class="best-practice">
                <h4>✓ Use semantic names</h4>
                <p>Always use the <code>z()</code> function with meaningful layer names. Never hardcode z-index values.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="best-practice">
                <h4>✓ Respect the hierarchy</h4>
                <p>Follow the layer order: canvas → content → interface → modal → guides. Don't skip layers without
                    reason.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="best-practice">
                <h4>✓ Use offsets sparingly</h4>
                <p>When you need relative stacking, use small offsets (+1 to +10). If you need larger gaps, consider
                    adding
                    a custom layer.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="best-practice">
                <h4>✓ Document custom layers</h4>
                <p>If you add custom layers to <code>$layer_map</code>, document their purpose and position in the
                    stack.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="best-practice">
                <h4>✗ Avoid z-index: 9999</h4>
                <p>The "make it bigger" approach leads to unmaintainable code. Use the layer system instead.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="best-practice">
                <h4>✗ Don't create stacking context chaos</h4>
                <p>Remember that <code>position: relative/absolute/fixed</code> with z-index creates new stacking
                    contexts.
                    Plan accordingly.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Stacking Contexts -->
    <section>
        <h2>Understanding Stacking Contexts</h2>

        <p>Z-index only works within a stacking context. Several CSS properties create new stacking contexts:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><code>position</code> (relative, absolute, fixed, sticky) with z-index other than auto</li>
            <li><code>opacity</code> less than 1</li>
            <li><code>transform</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>perspective</code></li>
            <li><code>isolation: isolate</code></li>
            <li>Flex/Grid items with z-index other than auto</li>
        </ul>

        <p>A child element cannot escape its parent's stacking context. If a modal is inside a container with
            <code>z-index: 1</code>, setting the modal to <code>z-index: 9999</code> won't place it above a sibling with
            <code>z-index: 2</code>.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block"><span class="comment">// Problem: Modal trapped in stacking context</span>
            .sidebar {
            position: relative;
            z-index: 1; <span class="comment">// Creates stacking context</span>
            }

            .sidebar .modal {
            z-index: z("modal"); <span class="comment">// 7000, but still below main content!</span>
            }

            <span class="comment">// Solution: Move modal outside or use isolation</span>
            .sidebar {
            position: relative;
            <span class="comment">// No z-index, no stacking context</span>
            }

            .main-content {
            isolation: isolate; <span class="comment">// Contains its own stacking</span>
            }
        </div>

        <div class="alert">
            <p class="alert__title">Key Insight</p>
            <p>The layer system provides consistent z-index values, but you still need to manage stacking contexts.
                Place
                modals and overlays at the root level of your DOM when possible.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

</div>

{%- endblock %}
